The fact table contains all \(3 \times 2 = 6\) combinations of the dimension values, so the base table has exactly six rows.

The operator `GROUP BY CUBE(D1,D2)` materializes every possible aggregation level for the two dimensions:

- the six finest-grained groups (one for each concrete pair \((D1,D2)\));
- the three groups obtained by aggregating over \(D2\) only (one for each value of \(D1\), with \(D2\) replaced by NULL);
- the two groups obtained by aggregating over \(D1\) only (one for each value of \(D2\), with \(D1\) replaced by NULL);
- the single grand-total group (both dimensions replaced by NULL).

These twelve groups are distinct because the NULL placeholders occupy different attribute positions; consequently the SELECT statement returns exactly twelve rows.

(The same count can be obtained from the closed-form expression \((3+1)\times(2+1)=12\).)

FINAL_ANSWER: D

